1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,210
How did France

2
00:00:01,230 --> 00:00:02,010
beat an army

3
00:00:02,030 --> 00:00:03,390
ten times its size,

4
00:00:03,410 --> 00:00:05,450
and still lose India?

5
00:00:05,470 --> 00:00:07,530
In 1746,

6
00:00:07,550 --> 00:00:08,250
about a thousand

7
00:00:08,270 --> 00:00:10,007
French-led soldiers

8
00:00:10,027 --> 00:00:10,650
beat an army

9
00:00:10,670 --> 00:00:11,730
of ten thousand

10
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:13,410
in southern India.

11
00:00:13,430 --> 00:00:13,730
Within a few years,

12
00:00:13,590 --> 00:00:15,410
a French governor

13
00:00:15,430 --> 00:00:16,416
was choosing

14
00:00:16,436 --> 00:00:16,990
who sat on

15
00:00:17,010 --> 00:00:18,430
India's thrones.

16
00:00:18,450 --> 00:00:21,050
Yet in May 1766,

17
00:00:21,070 --> 00:00:21,810
France executed

18
00:00:21,830 --> 00:00:23,350
its own general,

19
00:00:23,370 --> 00:00:24,532
the Comte de Lally,

20
00:00:24,552 --> 00:00:25,850
for losing it all.

21
00:00:25,870 --> 00:00:27,257
So what went wrong,

22
00:00:27,277 --> 00:00:28,170
between almost winning

23
00:00:28,190 --> 00:00:29,875
and losing everything?

24
00:00:29,895 --> 00:00:31,050
The answer wasn't

25
00:00:31,070 --> 00:00:32,300
a battle.

26
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:34,262
It was a supply line.

27
00:00:35,790 --> 00:00:36,430
The French were

28
00:00:36,450 --> 00:00:37,410
the last Europeans

29
00:00:37,430 --> 00:00:38,662
to arrive,

30
00:00:38,682 --> 00:00:39,330
so they had to

31
00:00:39,350 --> 00:00:40,450
move fast.

32
00:00:40,470 --> 00:00:42,350
In 1664,

33
00:00:42,370 --> 00:00:43,690
King Louis XIV's

34
00:00:43,710 --> 00:00:44,810
ambitious minister,

35
00:00:44,830 --> 00:00:46,110
Colbert,

36
00:00:46,130 --> 00:00:47,010
created the French

37
00:00:47,030 --> 00:00:48,708
East India Company,

38
00:00:48,728 --> 00:00:49,570
a 50-year

39
00:00:49,590 --> 00:00:49,950
monopoly

40
00:00:49,970 --> 00:00:52,429
on eastern trade.

41
00:00:52,449 --> 00:00:52,970
But it wasted

42
00:00:52,990 --> 00:00:54,610
its first years,

43
00:00:54,630 --> 00:00:55,430
much of its money,

44
00:00:55,450 --> 00:00:57,044
on Madagascar.

45
00:00:57,064 --> 00:00:57,630
So it turned

46
00:00:57,650 --> 00:00:58,972
to India.

47
00:00:58,992 --> 00:01:00,524
In 1668,

48
00:01:00,544 --> 00:01:01,550
François Caron

49
00:01:01,570 --> 00:01:02,570
opened a factory

50
00:01:02,590 --> 00:01:03,850
at Surat,

51
00:01:03,870 --> 00:01:05,130
a year later,

52
00:01:05,150 --> 00:01:06,150
an Armenian merchant

53
00:01:06,170 --> 00:01:07,290
in French service,

54
00:01:07,310 --> 00:01:08,790
Marcara,

55
00:01:08,810 --> 00:01:09,750
won trading rights

56
00:01:09,770 --> 00:01:10,450
from the Sultan

57
00:01:10,470 --> 00:01:11,670
of Golconda,

58
00:01:11,690 --> 00:01:12,470
opening the way

59
00:01:12,490 --> 00:01:14,210
to Masulipatnam.

60
00:01:14,230 --> 00:01:14,990
The French had

61
00:01:15,010 --> 00:01:16,489
a foothold.

62
00:01:16,509 --> 00:01:17,550
What they still lacked

63
00:01:17,570 --> 00:01:19,065
was a home.

64
00:01:20,485 --> 00:01:20,870
That came

65
00:01:20,890 --> 00:01:22,850
in 1673,

66
00:01:22,870 --> 00:01:24,850
when Sher Khan Lodi,

67
00:01:24,870 --> 00:01:25,570
a governor under

68
00:01:25,590 --> 00:01:27,665
the Sultan of Bijapur,

69
00:01:27,685 --> 00:01:28,750
granted them a site

70
00:01:28,770 --> 00:01:31,090
on the Coromandel coast.

71
00:01:31,110 --> 00:01:32,150
Pondicherry grew there

72
00:01:32,170 --> 00:01:34,170
from 1674,

73
00:01:34,190 --> 00:01:35,130
François Martin

74
00:01:35,150 --> 00:01:36,070
turned it into

75
00:01:36,090 --> 00:01:36,530
the heart

76
00:01:36,550 --> 00:01:38,135
of French India.

77
00:01:38,155 --> 00:01:39,070
But France's wars

78
00:01:39,090 --> 00:01:39,750
in Europe

79
00:01:39,770 --> 00:01:41,350
kept following it.

80
00:01:41,370 --> 00:01:43,210
So in 1693,

81
00:01:43,230 --> 00:01:43,690
the Dutch

82
00:01:43,710 --> 00:01:45,450
captured Pondicherry,

83
00:01:45,470 --> 00:01:47,110
only a peace treaty

84
00:01:47,130 --> 00:01:48,110
got it back.

85
00:01:48,130 --> 00:01:48,930
So when the company

86
00:01:48,950 --> 00:01:49,570
was rebuilt

87
00:01:49,590 --> 00:01:51,628
in the 1720s,

88
00:01:51,648 --> 00:01:51,948
it built

89
00:01:51,770 --> 00:01:53,222
a safety net:

90
00:01:53,242 --> 00:01:53,970
French islands

91
00:01:53,990 --> 00:01:55,870
in the Indian Ocean,

92
00:01:55,890 --> 00:01:56,730
Mauritius

93
00:01:56,750 --> 00:01:58,250
and Réunion.

94
00:01:58,270 --> 00:01:59,190
That safety net

95
00:01:59,210 --> 00:02:00,570
was about to become

96
00:02:00,590 --> 00:02:01,699
a weapon.

97
00:02:03,130 --> 00:02:05,090
But in 1746,

98
00:02:05,110 --> 00:02:06,790
Europe went to war again.

99
00:02:06,810 --> 00:02:07,510
A French fleet

100
00:02:07,530 --> 00:02:08,810
from Mauritius,

101
00:02:08,830 --> 00:02:10,295
under La Bourdonnais,

102
00:02:10,315 --> 00:02:11,190
sailed to India

103
00:02:11,210 --> 00:02:12,530
and captured Madras

104
00:02:12,550 --> 00:02:13,710
from the English.

105
00:02:13,730 --> 00:02:14,730
The French had promised

106
00:02:14,750 --> 00:02:16,410
Madras to the Nawab

107
00:02:16,430 --> 00:02:17,985
of the Carnatic.

108
00:02:18,005 --> 00:02:19,085
But they kept it,

109
00:02:19,105 --> 00:02:19,810
so the Nawab

110
00:02:19,830 --> 00:02:20,530
sent an army

111
00:02:20,550 --> 00:02:21,972
to throw them out.

112
00:02:21,992 --> 00:02:23,390
At the Adyar river,

113
00:02:23,410 --> 00:02:24,170
about a thousand

114
00:02:24,190 --> 00:02:25,090
French soldiers

115
00:02:25,110 --> 00:02:26,732
and trained sepoys

116
00:02:26,752 --> 00:02:28,830
faced some ten thousand,

117
00:02:28,850 --> 00:02:29,750
and won.

118
00:02:29,770 --> 00:02:30,290
Every European

119
00:02:30,310 --> 00:02:31,630
in India learned

120
00:02:31,650 --> 00:02:33,042
the same lesson:

121
00:02:33,062 --> 00:02:34,550
a small, drilled army

122
00:02:34,570 --> 00:02:35,130
could beat

123
00:02:35,150 --> 00:02:36,482
a far bigger one.

124
00:02:36,502 --> 00:02:38,130
A treaty in Europe

125
00:02:38,150 --> 00:02:39,550
handed Madras back

126
00:02:39,570 --> 00:02:40,896
to the English.

127
00:02:40,916 --> 00:02:41,770
But one Frenchman

128
00:02:41,790 --> 00:02:42,730
had learned

129
00:02:42,750 --> 00:02:44,176
something bigger.

130
00:02:45,596 --> 00:02:46,290
His name was

131
00:02:46,310 --> 00:02:48,336
Joseph François Dupleix,

132
00:02:48,356 --> 00:02:49,030
his idea

133
00:02:49,050 --> 00:02:50,350
was simple:

134
00:02:50,370 --> 00:02:50,870
why conquer

135
00:02:50,890 --> 00:02:52,356
India's thrones,

136
00:02:52,376 --> 00:02:53,150
when you can choose

137
00:02:53,170 --> 00:02:54,390
who sits on them?

138
00:02:54,410 --> 00:02:55,550
So when rival princes

139
00:02:55,570 --> 00:02:56,830
fought over Hyderabad

140
00:02:56,850 --> 00:02:58,546
and the Carnatic,

141
00:02:58,566 --> 00:02:59,250
Dupleix backed

142
00:02:59,270 --> 00:03:00,310
Muzaffar Jang

143
00:03:00,330 --> 00:03:02,186
and Chanda Sahib,

144
00:03:02,206 --> 00:03:03,110
the English backed

145
00:03:03,130 --> 00:03:04,290
their rivals.

146
00:03:04,310 --> 00:03:06,290
So in 1749,

147
00:03:06,310 --> 00:03:07,058
at Ambur,

148
00:03:07,078 --> 00:03:08,875
Dupleix's side won,

149
00:03:08,895 --> 00:03:10,370
the Nawab Anwar-ud-din

150
00:03:10,390 --> 00:03:11,386
was killed.

151
00:03:11,406 --> 00:03:12,290
Therefore Dupleix

152
00:03:12,310 --> 00:03:13,370
was made governor

153
00:03:13,390 --> 00:03:14,650
of the Mughal lands

154
00:03:14,670 --> 00:03:16,470
south of the Krishna,

155
00:03:16,490 --> 00:03:17,390
a French army

156
00:03:17,410 --> 00:03:18,390
under Bussy

157
00:03:18,410 --> 00:03:20,210
stayed at Hyderabad,

158
00:03:20,230 --> 00:03:20,730
paid for

159
00:03:20,750 --> 00:03:21,350
by the ruler

160
00:03:21,370 --> 00:03:22,481
it protected.

161
00:03:22,501 --> 00:03:24,612
It was a brilliant plan.

162
00:03:24,632 --> 00:03:25,770
But the English had

163
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:27,090
a young clerk

164
00:03:27,110 --> 00:03:28,596
watching closely.

165
00:03:30,016 --> 00:03:31,690
In 1751,

166
00:03:31,710 --> 00:03:32,310
that clerk,

167
00:03:32,330 --> 00:03:33,686
Robert Clive,

168
00:03:33,706 --> 00:03:36,110
took the capital, Arcot,

169
00:03:36,130 --> 00:03:36,750
with about

170
00:03:36,770 --> 00:03:38,176
500 men,

171
00:03:38,196 --> 00:03:38,930
held it through

172
00:03:38,950 --> 00:03:40,837
a 52-day siege.

173
00:03:40,857 --> 00:03:41,250
Therefore

174
00:03:41,270 --> 00:03:42,370
Dupleix's princes

175
00:03:42,390 --> 00:03:44,470
started losing.

176
00:03:44,490 --> 00:03:45,417
So in 1752,

177
00:03:45,437 --> 00:03:47,210
Chanda Sahib

178
00:03:47,230 --> 00:03:47,890
was captured

179
00:03:47,910 --> 00:03:49,670
and put to death.

180
00:03:50,190 --> 00:03:51,650
And back in Paris,

181
00:03:51,670 --> 00:03:53,080
the company's directors

182
00:03:53,100 --> 00:03:54,230
adding up the cost

183
00:03:54,250 --> 00:03:56,064
of Dupleix's wars.

184
00:03:56,084 --> 00:03:57,890
So in 1754

185
00:03:57,910 --> 00:03:59,434
they called him home,

186
00:03:59,454 --> 00:04:00,750
his successor signed

187
00:04:00,770 --> 00:04:01,210
a truce

188
00:04:01,230 --> 00:04:02,730
with the English.

189
00:04:02,750 --> 00:04:03,830
France had walked away

190
00:04:03,850 --> 00:04:05,265
from the board.

191
00:04:05,285 --> 00:04:05,910
But the game

192
00:04:05,930 --> 00:04:07,594
was not over.

193
00:04:09,014 --> 00:04:10,110
When the Seven Years War

194
00:04:10,130 --> 00:04:11,170
began,

195
00:04:11,190 --> 00:04:11,650
France sent

196
00:04:11,670 --> 00:04:13,110
the Comte de Lally,

197
00:04:13,130 --> 00:04:15,030
a proud, desperate general,

198
00:04:15,050 --> 00:04:16,130
to finish the English

199
00:04:16,150 --> 00:04:17,050
for good.

200
00:04:17,070 --> 00:04:18,350
At first, he pushed them

201
00:04:18,370 --> 00:04:20,041
back toward Madras.

202
00:04:20,061 --> 00:04:20,890
But the English had

203
00:04:20,910 --> 00:04:22,241
something he didn't:

204
00:04:22,710 --> 00:04:23,010
Bengal,

205
00:04:22,710 --> 00:04:24,050
won at Plassey,

206
00:04:24,070 --> 00:04:25,250
sending them money,

207
00:04:25,270 --> 00:04:25,570
food

208
00:04:25,530 --> 00:04:26,510
and men.

209
00:04:26,530 --> 00:04:27,110
Lally's supplies

210
00:04:27,130 --> 00:04:27,830
had to come

211
00:04:27,850 --> 00:04:28,970
the long way,

212
00:04:28,990 --> 00:04:30,407
by sea from France.

213
00:04:30,427 --> 00:04:31,470
So on the 22nd

214
00:04:31,490 --> 00:04:33,180
of January,

215
00:04:33,200 --> 00:04:34,071
1760,

216
00:04:34,091 --> 00:04:34,710
at Wandiwash,

217
00:04:34,730 --> 00:04:36,310
Sir Eyre Coote,

218
00:04:36,330 --> 00:04:38,150
patient and well supplied,

219
00:04:38,170 --> 00:04:40,090
defeated him decisively.

220
00:04:40,110 --> 00:04:40,750
Lally retreated

221
00:04:40,770 --> 00:04:42,430
into Pondicherry

222
00:04:42,450 --> 00:04:43,190
held out for

223
00:04:43,210 --> 00:04:44,650
about eight months.

224
00:04:44,670 --> 00:04:45,830
Therefore, in January

225
00:04:45,850 --> 00:04:47,690
1761,

226
00:04:47,710 --> 00:04:49,289
the city surrendered.

227
00:04:49,309 --> 00:04:50,710
But France still needed

228
00:04:50,730 --> 00:04:52,270
someone to blame.

229
00:04:53,690 --> 00:04:54,690
The Treaty of Paris,

230
00:04:54,710 --> 00:04:56,900
in 1763,

231
00:04:56,920 --> 00:04:57,870
gave the French back

232
00:04:57,890 --> 00:04:59,310
their trading posts,

233
00:04:59,330 --> 00:05:00,741
but not their power.

234
00:05:00,761 --> 00:05:01,910
Clive himself said

235
00:05:01,930 --> 00:05:02,950
the English had done

236
00:05:02,970 --> 00:05:04,151
to the French

237
00:05:04,171 --> 00:05:05,470
exactly what the French

238
00:05:05,490 --> 00:05:05,970
had planned

239
00:05:05,990 --> 00:05:07,550
to do to them.

240
00:05:07,570 --> 00:05:09,090
Many historians call Plassey

241
00:05:09,110 --> 00:05:10,210
the turning point

242
00:05:10,230 --> 00:05:11,780
for British India.

243
00:05:11,800 --> 00:05:12,930
But it was Wandiwash

244
00:05:12,950 --> 00:05:14,250
that left the English

245
00:05:14,270 --> 00:05:15,870
with no European rival

246
00:05:15,890 --> 00:05:16,830
at all.

247
00:05:16,850 --> 00:05:17,290
So the blame

248
00:05:17,310 --> 00:05:19,290
fell on one man.

249
00:05:19,310 --> 00:05:19,930
Lally went home

250
00:05:19,950 --> 00:05:20,814
a prisoner,

251
00:05:20,834 --> 00:05:22,450
five years later,

252
00:05:22,470 --> 00:05:24,430
France executed him.

253
00:05:24,450 --> 00:05:25,230
But was the loss

254
00:05:25,250 --> 00:05:27,063
really his fault?

255
00:05:28,483 --> 00:05:29,110
Yet the French

256
00:05:29,130 --> 00:05:30,932
never fully left.

257
00:05:30,952 --> 00:05:31,770
Chandernagore

258
00:05:31,790 --> 00:05:32,650
joined India

259
00:05:32,670 --> 00:05:34,210
in 1950,

260
00:05:34,230 --> 00:05:34,910
and Pondicherry

261
00:05:34,930 --> 00:05:36,610
and the other French towns

262
00:05:36,630 --> 00:05:39,032
followed in 1954,

263
00:05:39,052 --> 00:05:39,690
after a vote

264
00:05:39,710 --> 00:05:40,916
at Kizhur.

265
00:05:40,936 --> 00:05:41,810
So what really

266
00:05:41,830 --> 00:05:43,490
decided India?

267
00:05:43,510 --> 00:05:44,090
France had the

268
00:05:44,110 --> 00:05:45,790
smartest idea:

269
00:05:45,810 --> 00:05:46,210
rule through

270
00:05:46,230 --> 00:05:47,550
India's thrones,

271
00:05:47,570 --> 00:05:48,616
not around them.

272
00:05:48,636 --> 00:05:49,230
But ideas

273
00:05:49,250 --> 00:05:50,406
need money,

274
00:05:50,426 --> 00:05:51,510
a company that waits

275
00:05:51,530 --> 00:05:52,150
for orders

276
00:05:52,170 --> 00:05:53,466
from Paris

277
00:05:53,486 --> 00:05:54,350
can't outlast one

278
00:05:54,370 --> 00:05:55,590
that pays for itself

279
00:05:55,610 --> 00:05:56,830
from Bengal.

280
00:05:56,850 --> 00:05:57,490
The English took

281
00:05:57,510 --> 00:05:59,110
Dupleix's idea,

282
00:05:59,130 --> 00:06:00,890
added Bengal's money,

283
00:06:00,910 --> 00:06:01,810
and built an empire

284
00:06:01,830 --> 00:06:04,939
with both.
